{"candidates":[{"claim":"Separation of authoring and checking engines is unnecessary if 'independence' is defined as information-theoretic blindness to the author's latent reasoning path.","counterconditions":["The checker can recover the author's latent state through the linguistic style of the final claim (steganographic leakage).","The shared priors are so dominant that any two paths to the same conclusion are identical."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Functional separation is achieved by restricting the checker's input to the final claim only, preventing the 'Echo Chamber' effect (SCR_001) where the checker merely validates the author's internal logic chain. By forcing the checker to reconstruct the proof from first principles without access to the author's intermediate state, the system simulates two independent agents regardless of shared weights.","optional_refs":["042f7635ef946e95d91bc6cdb192f4882ece05c12d99375eb9e40b25ba2c3ace"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:25a17b6dcb7918c121f9d4e245785d3966449f9e3475cbdb53272d5bec6b364d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"Independent verification is impossible within a single model regardless of prompting, as the 'checker' is merely a different projection of the same biased manifold.","counterconditions":["The model possesses a symbolic execution engine (code interpreter) that operates outside the probabilistic manifold.","The prompt-induced state shift is sufficiently large to move the activation into a region with uncorrelated error properties."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'error terms' of a single model are perfectly correlated (SCR_002); any 'critique' mode (SRC_003) is a simulated adversariality that follows the same underlying probability distribution. Genuine verification requires a 'collision' of two different training histories to expose blind spots that are systemic to one architecture.","optional_refs":["1903fca7213c01eae0acd73b42bbf730a599034960566767ee9c784aaada8ca6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:78e494a652894bcd94dfd8f3c373f47cb0b3aedb831933146b6250d9279c7889"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"claim":"Verification is distinguished from restatement by the 'Lexical Divergence Metric': the ratio of unique evidentiary citations in the check versus the authoring phase.","counterconditions":["The problem domain has only one possible set of supporting evidence.","The checker uses synonyms to mask restatement of the same logical path."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the checker identifies the same conclusion using a disjoint set of source artifacts or different logical axioms than the author, it has performed an independent reconstruction. Restatement is identified by a lexical overlap > 0.8 (SCR_001) in the justification phase. One must count the intersection of cited source IDs between the author's and checker's proofs.","optional_refs":["2f61dd59e4153c08a0d04f21378569b0d3bd8ffc5048b0360b18de3ff5211f39"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:c8027f5b3c11f52dc32c56bcae14c1c1893e118460de8b41822a34bc8a583ca8","sha256:8c712193073f2ee5e5783849abe372a2879a946e1d67aaad73613154ee727e59"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"claim":"Separation is a red herring; the only valid 'independent verification' is the execution of a formal specification derived from the claim.","counterconditions":["The authoring engine also wrote the test code, creating a circular dependency (SRC_008).","The claim is non-computable."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Verification is not a property of the 'engine' but of the 'grounding'. By transforming the claim into a testable program (SRC_006), the 'checker' (even if it is the same model) delegates the truth-value to an external, deterministic runtime. The evidence is the binary pass/fail of the executed code, which is independent of the model's training history.","optional_refs":["bdc9e4af91e13df0c1352c8f077d6c8f06c68da0bb4cf8865e6c573ef0edeae6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"claim":"Independence is achieved through 'Stochastic Perturbation' (Entropy Injection) rather than engine separation.","counterconditions":["The noise introduces new errors rather than exposing existing ones.","The model's output is too constrained by top-p sampling to allow meaningful divergence."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"By increasing the temperature or injecting random noise into the checker's latent state (SCR_004), the system breaks the deterministic correlation of errors. Verification is proven if the result is stable across N high-entropy samples, whereas restatement would collapse or fluctuate wildly under noise.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["sha256:4b3a95be73cb742824d14057741920a39aa80d9e7b44ae42f2f51361fe83ab7f","sha256:c7af19824dcec3347ec254be01e5537a1d0eee9eed267aabea52d5c5853e9967"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.2}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["sha256:25a17b6dcb7918c121f9d4e245785d3966449f9e3475cbdb53272d5bec6b364d","sha256:78e494a652894bcd94dfd8f3c373f47cb0b3aedb831933146b6250d9279c7889","sha256:c8027f5b3c11f52dc32c56bcae14c1c1893e118460de8b41822a34bc8a583ca8","sha256:8c712193073f2ee5e5783849abe372a2879a946e1d67aaad73613154ee727e59","sha256:4b3a95be73cb742824d14057741920a39aa80d9e7b44ae42f2f51361fe83ab7f","sha256:c7af19824dcec3347ec254be01e5537a1d0eee9eed267aabea52d5c5853e9967"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Counter-intuitive thought: What if the 'checking engine' is actually MORE likely to be biased if it is a different model? If Model B is slightly weaker than Model A, it might 'verify' a wrong claim simply because it cannot find the error, whereas Model A (checking itself) might have the specific internal 'memory' of the error it made. This suggests that 'independence' might actually decrease reliability in specific failure modes.","why_keep_this":"Challenges the axiom that separate engines are always better."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"How do we distinguish between a checker that is 'independent' and a checker that is simply 'wrong' but confident?","related_refs":["SCR_002"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}